=== Report ===
A review of national wheat prices over the 2011/12 and 2012/13 marketing years reveals clear patterns and noteworthy price movements across different classes of wheat. The data tracks average prices received by farmers, distinguishing between all wheat, winter wheat, durum, and other spring wheat varieties, providing a monthly breakdown from June through May for each year.

Examining the figures presented in ## Sheet: Sheet1

| Table 5--Wheat: National average price received by farmers (dollars per bushel) 1/, 6/14/2013   | Unnamed: 1   | Unnamed: 2   | Unnamed: 3   | Unnamed: 4   | Unnamed: 5   | Unnamed: 6   | Unnamed: 7   | Unnamed: 8   |
|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-------------|:-------------|:-------------|:-------------|:-------------|:-------------|:-------------|:-------------|
| Month                                                                                           | All wheat    | nan          | Winter       | nan          | Durum        | nan          | Other spring | nan          |
| nan                                                                                             | 2011/12      | 2012/13      | 2011/12      | 2012/13      | 2011/12      | 2012/13      | 2011/12      | 2012/13      |
| June                                                                                            | 7.41         | 6.7          | 7.13         | 6.54         | 9.18         | 8.31         | 9.26         | 7.78         |
| July                                                                                            | 7.1          | 7.93         | 6.77         | 7.79         | 10.2         | 8.6          | 8.45         | 8.53         |
| August                                                                                          | 7.59         | 8.04         | 7.27         | 7.92         | 10.2         | 7.7          | 8.28         | 8.27         |
| September                                                                                       | 7.54         | 8.27         | 7            | 8.25         | 10.8         | 7.74         | 8.09         | 8.38         |
| October                                                                                         | 7.27         | 8.38         | 6.53         | 8.33         | 9.6          | 7.61         | 8.19         | 8.56         |
| November                                                                                        | 7.3          | 8.46         | 6.44         | 8.38         | 10.3         | 8.16         | 8.43         | 8.65         |
| December                                                                                        | 7.2          | 8.29         | 6.41         | 8.15         | 10.3         | 8.31         | 8.25         | 8.46         |
| January                                                                                         | 7.05         | 8.12         | 6.57         | 8.01         | 8.84         | 8.24         | 8.09         | 8.33         |
| February                                                                                        | 7.1          | 7.97         | 6.68         | 7.87         | 8.98         | 8.19         | 8.01         | 8.1          |
| March                                                                                           | 7.2          | 7.78         | 6.7          | 7.62         | 8.39         | 8.12         | 8.04         | 7.95         |
| April                                                                                           | 7.11         | 7.71         | 6.47         | 7.52         | 9.22         | 8.01         | 7.96         | 7.9          |
| May                                                                                             | 6.67         | 7.51         | 6.42         | 7.37         | 8.95         | 7.92         | 7.93         | 7.9          |
| 1/ Preliminary mid-month, weighted-average price for current month.                             | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          |
| Source: USDA, National Agricultural Statistics Service, Agricultural Prices.                    |              |              |              |              |              |              |              |              |
| Date run: 6/12/2013                                                                             | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          | nan          |
, one observes that prices for all wheat began the 2011/12 marketing year at $7.41 per bushel in June, before declining to $6.67 by the following May. Winter wheat, following a similar trajectory, started at $7.13 and fell to $6.42 within the same period. Durum wheat, traditionally a premium product, commenced at $9.18 and ended at $8.95, while other spring wheat began at $9.26 and concluded the year at $7.93.

The subsequent 2012/13 period illustrated both volatility and upward movement. Across all wheat, prices ranged from $6.70 in June to a seasonal high of $8.46 in November, before settling at $7.51 in May. Winter wheat saw a substantial climb, peaking in November at $8.38, while durum wheat, although still pricing above most other varieties, experienced considerable volatility—falling from $8.31 in June, dropping further to $7.61 in October, before recovering to $8.31 in December and hovering near $7.92 in the season's final month. The other spring wheat category echoed this fluctuation, with prices rising from $7.78 in June up to $8.65 by November, and ultimately closing at $7.90 in May.

Seasonal factors and market dynamics manifest in monthly variations. Typically, wheat prices tend to rise through the late summer and autumn months, likely reflecting post-harvest supply adjustments and international market developments, before gradually moderating throughout the winter and spring. The sharpest price increases in 2012/13 are notable in the transition from June to November—a period marked by concerns over global supply and possibly currency effects.

These monthly averages across wheat classes highlight both the premium associated with durum and other spring varieties, and the relative stability of winter wheat pricing. To illustrate how such price differentials can impact farm revenues, consider a hypothetical table comparing revenue per acre given average yields for each wheat type at both low and high season prices. Such an analysis, while not based on the original data, would reflect the importance of both price movements and yield differentials in producer income.

Overall, the two-year price trajectory underscores a period of relative strength for U.S. wheat producers, with 2012/13 marking a particularly robust marketing year. The variation between wheat classes and across months reflects a combination of domestic and global influences, contributing to an environment where responsive marketing and crop selection hold significant financial consequences for growers.

