Please extract the speed distribution data (counts for each speed band: 30 mph, 40 mph, 50 mph, 60 mph, 70 mph) from Sheet1 into JSON using the provided schema.

=== Report ===
A focused examination of speed distribution over a defined range of velocities reveals a pronounced concentration of events at the lower end of the spectrum. The figures in ## Sheet: Sheet1

|   30 mph |   40mph |   50mph |   60mph |   70mph |
|---------:|--------:|--------:|--------:|--------:|
|       45 |       7 |       3 |       0 |       0 |
 demonstrate that a substantial majority—45 out of a total of 55 recorded instances—occur at 30 mph. As the speed increases, the frequency of occurrences declines sharply: only 7 events are recorded at 40 mph, with further steep decreases to 3 at 50 mph, and none at either 60 mph or 70 mph.

This immediate drop-off signals a behavioral or operational threshold at or just above 30 mph, beyond which activity becomes rare. The near absence of occurrences above 40 mph suggests either external constraints—such as speed limits, vehicle capability, or regulatory controls—or a systematic preference for lower speeds in the environment under consideration.

To contextualize these results, suppose we envisage a hypothetical distribution in which speed selection is equally probable across categories. In such a fabricated scenario, we would expect a table with roughly uniform counts across all speed bands, in stark contrast to the actual distribution’s skewness. This disparity underscores the non-random, constrained nature of real-world speed choices in the observed context.

The data thus points to a regime dominated by low-speed operation, with strong avoidance or prohibition of higher-speed bands, highlighting the critical role of either policy or circumstance in shaping movement patterns.

