The staffing counts are stratified distinctly at multiple organizational layers—individual educational agencies, counties, and the state—so as to avoid duplication of personnel who may be employed across different entities within the system. Such granular categorization affords clarity when comparing workforce size and composition within and across educational administrative units. Staff members’ education levels are categorized on a scale ranging from less than a high school diploma through doctoral degrees, allowing for identification of qualifications prevalent among this workforce.

Contractual salary data primarily pertain to the active 2010-11 school year; however, exceptions exist where figures reflect prior contract years due to differing contract expiration dates. This nuance is essential when interpreting salary trends or drawing comparisons among agencies. Salary figures represent commitments for the full contract year specified, ensuring that comparisons remain aligned to the intended compensation period.

Within this framework, public education employs a multi-tiered system of institutions, each characterized by distinct administrative codes such as SD (School District), CS (Charter School), CTC (Career Technical Center or Area Vocational-Technical School), IU (Intermediate Unit), and SJCI (State Juvenile Correctional Institute). This classification supports targeted workforce assessments and policy considerations tailored to institutional type.

