This report presents an analytical review of pricing dynamics as they relate to unit cost adjustments across tiered volume quantities. The data reflects the impact of incremental multipliers on unit prices as quantities increase, highlighting how cost per unit evolves in relation to ordered volume.

At the foundation, a base unit cost of 0.65 is established, serving as the starting reference point. As unit quantities rise in increments of 100, the pricing model applies a series of multipliers to the initial 100 units, effectively reducing the incremental cost per additional 100-unit block. These multipliers decrease progressively: starting from 0 for the first 100 units, then adjusting to 0.5 for the next 100 units, 0.25 for the following 100, and diminishing further to 0.18 and 0.13 in subsequent increments. This diminishing multiplier scheme suggests a volume discount structure, encouraging larger orders through decreasing marginal costs.

Sales quantity thresholds are clearly defined: from zero to 100 units, 101 to 200, 201 to 300, continuing similarly up to quantities over 500 units. For quantities surpassing 500, the pattern indicates additional units are billed under separate total calculations, though specific incremental multipliers beyond 500 units are less explicitly noted.

