Complementing the action codes, telephone interaction codes catalog both outgoing and incoming call outcomes. Outgoing call codes like OUTAGR and OUTFAIL distinguish between successful negotiations and those requiring escalation, respectively. Incoming call codes—INAGR, INMON, and INPAY—document responses from account holders, ranging from new payment agreement setups to routine account payments with no arrears issues. Payment handling pathways are also specified, with direct references to card payments (PAY), the absence of payment (NOPAY), and direct debit arrangements (DD, DDPAY).

Hierarchical priority is assigned within the arrears context as well. Priorities span from “1 - High” to “6 - Low.” Intriguingly, the highest arrears priorities are mapped to analogies from emergency response vehicles—police cars, ambulances, and fire engines—while lower priorities correspond to standard modes of transportation, including trains, buses, and bikes. This alignment provides intuitive shorthand for urgency and action sequencing within arrears management.

