The analysis focuses on a selection of frozen dessert offerings and their associated toppings, highlighting calorie content alongside pricing structures differentiated by customer type and product variety. This evaluation encompasses a range of ice cream flavors, premium options, nuts, syrups, and sprinkles—with an emphasis on comparing available choices and relative costs to members and guests.

Among the core ice cream selections, flavors such as Vanilla, Chocolate, and Pineapple demonstrate moderate calorie levels, generally spanning from 225 to 325 calories per serving. Notably, Pineapple lacks pricing information, suggesting it may be temporarily unavailable or subject to separate valuation. Premium ice cream variants, including Mint Chocolate Chip and Caramel Pecan Swirl, stand out with higher caloric values ranging from 375 to 400 calories and premium pricing that exceeds standard varieties, reinforcing their positioned exclusivity.

The topping options present a stark contrast in both calorie counts and pricing dynamics. Nut-based toppings such as Peanuts and Pecans maintain low-calorie profiles around 50 calories with slight price variances between standard and sugar-free options. Syrups, particularly those with rich flavors like Dark Chocolate Pecan Fudge, push the calorie boundary over 400 in some cases, yet their cost to customers remains relatively modest, especially for sugar-free alternatives which are less consistently priced or sometimes unavailable. The sprinkle category rounds out the choices with minimal caloric impact and a small premium for sugar-free coconut sprinkles versus their standard counterparts.

Pricing trends reveal a consistent premium charged to non-members across almost all items, typically ranging from $0.90 to $1.00 more than member prices for ice cream flavors. Premium ice creams show a clear upward price trajectory with members paying $4 to $5 and some guest prices unspecified, hinting at potentially varied or exclusive access. Topping prices vary less dramatically, with sugar-free varieties occasionally matching or slightly exceeding standard prices, perhaps reflecting specialized ingredients or production costs.

