A close review reveals that scenarios are broadly distributed across the Western, Eastern, and Mediterranean fronts. Engagements range from isolated actions on a single day to protracted operations spanning upwards of two weeks. Both the Allies and Axis powers are represented, each achieving victories and defeats under varying circumstances.

The simulation corresponding to the Western front, set on December 18, 1944, features an Allied victory with a decisive score margin of 6 to 3. This encounter, likely modeled on the closing phases of the Battle of the Bulge, suggests effective Allied tactics and a well-executed operational plan. Conversely, battles on the Eastern front, such as the iterations labeled “Battle of Kalatch,” display alternation in success: though both battles involve the same opponent, “niko the great,” the first results in an Axis victory while the latter is claimed by the Allies. Both sides achieved a winning score of 6, but the Allies were pushed closer, conceding 4 points to the Axis’ earlier 3, indicative of a more closely fought rematch.

The Mediterranean theater is represented by a pair of back-to-back engagements on November 25, 1941. “Dug in at [Person_2],” contested by “tommikins” as the opponent, ended in split defeats for both sides—first the Axis, then the Allies. Here, the scores were 5–6 and 4–6, respectively, underlining consistently narrow margins and robust defensive play.

