=== Report ===
An evaluation of hourly wage indices across Australian states and territories between 1998 and 2011 reveals notable patterns and regional variations. This examination draws on indexed measures of total hourly rates of pay excluding bonuses, covering both private and public employment sectors and all industries collectively. The data spans financial years ending in June for each year in the 14-year range, thereby allowing for a longitudinal perspective on wage trends.

Throughout this period, all regions demonstrated some degree of wage growth as measured by the indexed values, though the extent and consistency of that growth varied. The national summary reflects aggregate movement, which provides a baseline for assessing individual state and territory performance.

A key feature of the data is that each jurisdiction maintains its own trajectory within the overall upward trend of hourly pay rates. New South Wales, Victoria, and Queensland — some of the most populous states — offer particularly informative contrast in index level progression. Other territories, including Tasmania and the Northern Territory, present smaller scales of employment but nonetheless contribute important insights regarding labor price dynamics in less populous areas.

The coverage across both public and private sectors and all industries ensures the indices capture broad wage behavior unaffected by sectorial or industry-specific distortions. This holistic view is crucial for policymakers and economic analysts seeking to understand real labor cost growth rather than isolated compensation components.

