Could you extract the weekly top set data for squat, bench, and deadlift, from the analysis of the powerlifting training cycle, and present it as JSON using the chart structure provided? Please follow the given format for weeks and lifts in your output.

=== Report ===
This report presents an analysis of a progressive strength training regimen over a seven-week period, focusing on high-level powerlifting preparations with attention to bench press, squat, and deadlift sessions. The structured routine demonstrates a methodical approach toward peaking for a competition, with clear progression in volume and intensity, as well as targeted goal setting for attempted lifts.

Throughout the weeks, the sessions highlight both raw and equipped lifting, with specific board presses and floor press variations for the bench, alongside regular inclusion of accessory exercises such as incline dumbbell press, pushdowns, and kennleys. Squat and deadlift sessions incorporate both traditional and reverse band modalities, signaling the athlete's emphasis on explosive strength and lockout power.

Between the week of April 18 and the week of June 13, the progression in load and repetition schemes can be observed in ## Sheet: Sheet1

| week of 4-18                                                  | week of 4-25                                                  |
|:--------------------------------------------------------------|:--------------------------------------------------------------|
| 405x2 full, 495x3 3board, 495x3 2board, incline dumbells 3x8  | 405x2 2x3@495 2board, pushdowns, kennleys                     |
| floor press, 3x6@295                                          | floor press 4x5@295                                           |
| Reverse Band Deads (blues) warm ups, 3x4@615                  | Reverse Band Deads (blues) 3x3@655                            |
| week of 5-2                                                   | week of 5-9                                                   |
| 4x5 @ 315 plus chains floor press, pushdowns, kennleys        | 1x3@ 495 3board, 1x2@ 585 3board, 1x3@635 1board, kennleys    |
| speed day 9x3@225 changing grips                              | speed day 6x3@225 changing grips                              |
| squat full gear 1x2@665, 1x2@775, 1x1@815                     | squat 4x1@ 705, 800, 830                                      |
| week of 5/16                                                  | week of 5/23                                                  |
| 1x3@495x3board 1x3@585@2board, 1x2@655@1board, 1x1@675@1board | 1x3@495x3board 1x3@585@2board, 1x2@655@1board, 1x2@675@1board |
| deads 3x3@ 605, 625, 645                                      | [Person_1] squats 3x2 705, 775, 800                           |
| week of 5/30                                                  | week of 6/6                                                   |
| 1x3@495x3board 1x3@585@2board, 1x2@655@1board, 1x1@675 chest  | 1x3@495x3board 1x2@585x2board, 1x2@655@1board, 1x1@700 chest  |
| deads 1x2@615 1x2@ 675                                        | squat 1x1 @705. (should be an easy opener for the meet.       |
| week of 6/13 rest up, then kick ass!                          | nan                                                           |
| nan                                                           | attempts goals                                                |
| 850-700-700=2250lbs                                           | squat: 705, 825, 850+                                         |
| nan                                                           | bench: 635, 670, 700                                          |
| nan                                                           | dead: 635, 675, 700                                           |
, detailing the transition from foundational work to high-intensity singles and triples. Notable milestones include ramping up squat intensities to over 800 pounds, bench press lockouts on heavy boards, and pulling heavy deadlifts with ascending loads.

Visualization of this periodized approach might resemble the following hypothetical chart summarizing weekly top set intensity by lift:

| Week        | Top Squat (lbs) | Top Bench (lbs) | Top Deadlift (lbs) |
|-------------|-----------------|-----------------|--------------------|
| 4/18        | 775             | 495 (3board)    | 615                |
| 4/25        | 800             | 495 (2board)    | 655                |
| 5/2         | 815             | 315 + chains    | N/A                |
| 5/9         | 830             | 635 (1board)    | N/A                |
| 5/16        | 675 (1board)    | 675 (1board)    | 645                |
| 5/23        | 800             | 675 (1board)    | N/A                |
| 5/30        | 675 (chest)     | 675 (chest)     | 675                |
| 6/6         | 705             | 700             | N/A                |

*Note: This illustrative table is provided to give a sense of max intensity focus by week. Actual training logs include numerous additional sets and variations.*

By the final entries, the program culminates in rest and recovery to facilitate peak performance for competition. Concretely defined attempt goals—such as a 705 lb opening squat, progressing to potential attempts of 825 and 850+ lbs, a bench press peaking at 700 lbs, and a 700 lb deadlift—reflect both ambition and realism, well-aligned with the preparatory work undertaken in preceding weeks.

Summing up, the lifter’s regimen balances progressive overload with technical refinement and strategic deloading, setting the stage for a strong meet performance with a targeted total in the 2250 lb range. Each training segment, as evidenced in the detailed weekly records, supports a disciplined march toward these competitive benchmarks.

