Geographically, staff members are located across a wide global footprint, including major metropolitan centers in Europe (London, Rome, Paris, Madrid), Asia (Beijing), North America (New York), and Oceania (Sydney). This distribution suggests a strategic global presence and perhaps a deployment approach that aligns specific business units and services with regional market demands.

Within service assignments, designations A, B, and C are seen across personnel, frequently correlating with their business and unit placements. For example, several members within business area D and unit A provide service A, while others in business areas E and F are more likely to be involved with service B or C. 

The staffing dates present an interesting progression: the earliest entry corresponds to a part-time role in Madrid in 2009, while the latest are clustered in 2011, predominantly full-time roles in London, Rome, Paris, and New York. This may reflect a shift toward more full-time engagements in recent years or a response to changing business needs.

