=== Report ===
This report examines recent trends in the United States soybean oil market, focusing on both supply and usage patterns over three sequential years. Soybean oil, an essential component of both the food industry and the renewable fuels sector, depends on several key factors: initial stock levels each marketing year, domestic production, imports, total supply, various categories of domestic disappearance, exports, and closing inventory.

A review of annual figures reveals that the beginning stock of soybean oil increased modestly from the 2011/12 to the 2012/13 marketing years, rising from about 2.43 to 2.54 billion pounds. However, the following year marked a sharp decline in opening stocks to 1.71 billion pounds. Domestic production stayed relatively stable across the period, fluctuating around 19.7 to 19.8 billion pounds, while imports contributed a consistently modest volume, ranging from 149 to 200 million pounds annually. Total supply echoed these trends, exceeding 21.6 billion pounds in each year under consideration.

Turning to usage, domestic disappearance was robust, staying in the range of 18.3 to 18.7 billion pounds. A significant portion of this was allocated to biodiesel production, generally near or exceeding 4.6 billion pounds each year, highlighting the ongoing role of soybean oil as a renewable fuel feedstock. Food use accounted for the majority segment of domestic consumption, which reflects the stable demand from the food processing industry.

Export performance varied, with a notable surge in 2012/13, when exports rose above 2.16 billion pounds, compared to 1.46 and 1.5 billion pounds in the other years. As a result, ending stocks showed marked year-on-year volatility, peaking at 2.54 billion pounds in 2011/12, dropping to 1.71 billion in 2012/13, and stabilizing at 1.58 billion in 2013/14. All these data points are summarized concisely in ## Sheet: Sheet1

