The subsequent period (21/02/2014 – 27/02/2014) saw a marked overall increase in estimator activity. Person_4’s workload spiked sharply to thirteen, while Franca’s count rose to fourteen—more than ten times her previous week’s assignments. Carmen and Person_3 also registered significant increases, with four and five estimators respectively. Kelly’s tally went up to six. In contrast, Person_2’s count dropped to one, suggesting a redistribution of tasks or shifting team priorities.

In the brief final period (28/02/2014 – 01/03/2014), task counts fell across the board. Person_2, Person_3, and Person_4 each handled one or two estimators, while Carmen, Franca, and Kelly were either unassigned or had no recorded activity.

These temporal trends point to a highly dynamic allocation of estimators among staff, possibly reflecting fluctuating project demands or shifts in team availability. The stark surge in assignment counts for select team members in late February, especially Franca and Person_4, raises questions about workload equity and resource planning. To illustrate how such fluctuations might benchmark against an evenly distributed task load, consider the hypothetical scenario in Table A below, which presumes uniform estimator assignments across all individuals and periods:

