=== Report ===
An analysis of recent voting activity reveals distinctive patterns in both voter turnout and party affiliation across multiple counties. The available figures provide parallel insights into ballots cast for each major party and the corresponding numbers of registered voters, allowing for a nuanced comparison of participation rates and party engagement.

A county-level breakdown of ballots cast in a recent election distinctly separates Republican and Democratic participation, further subdivided into regular and absentee ballots. For example, several counties—namely Belknap, Carroll, Cheshire, and Coos—demonstrate clear disparities between total ballots cast for each party. In Belknap, regular Republican ballots totaled over 4,100, substantially outnumbering their Democratic counterparts, whose regular ballots slightly exceeded 1,000. This trend of Republican ballots surpassing Democratic ballots carries through in other counties, although the degree of dominance and the ratio of absentee to regular ballots varies.

A closer look at absentee ballot usage reveals it remains a minor yet consistent component of overall turnout. Even in populous counties like Hillsborough and Rockingham, absentee ballots formed only a small fraction of the total. Notably, Hillsborough registered 594 absentee Republican ballots and 378 absentee Democratic ballots out of totals exceeding 21,000 and 11,000, respectively. Such numbers suggest that absentee voting, while present, did not play a decisive role relative to in-person voting during this cycle.

Integrating this snapshot of ballots cast with party registration offers valuable perspective. Voter rolls across counties reflect a range of party affiliations, with Republican, Democratic, and undeclared voters all prominently represented. For example, in Rockingham County, registered Republicans numbered over 65,000, Democrats more than 46,000, and undeclared voters also exceeded 65,000—underscoring the significant presence of unaffiliated voters. Similarly, Hillsborough, the most populous county represented, showed a large contingent of undeclared and Democratic voters in addition to a sizable Republican base.

Voter turnout, when considered as a percentage of party registration, introduces further complexity. To illustrate, consider the hypothetical table below, which compares turnout rates (ballots cast as a percentage of party-registered voters) for select counties:

| County        | Republican Turnout (%) | Democratic Turnout (%) |
