When considering employment type, the landscape is strikingly sparse. All formal employment categories—“Independent - Retail,” “Chain - Retail,” “Hospital,” “Consultant,” “Institutional - Other,” “Retired,” and “Unemployed”—show a recorded count of zero. Furthermore, for the majority of participants where job titles are listed, employment type and associated counts are either omitted or marked as “nan,” indicating missing or inapplicable information. This pattern reveals minimal to no active professional attachment for the represented roles, or alternatively, highlights a systematic data recording issue.

The high incidence of missing employment type data and the predominance of unique job titles point to either a group with diverse credentialing and limited current workforce participation or a data collection geared predominantly towards credential confirmation rather than employment tracking. A fabricated summary table, if provided for illustration, could show an idealized distribution by role type and employment status to highlight how concentrated sectors would be expected to appear in a typical professional cohort:

