Among the animals surveyed, birds are described as having a smell ranging from neutral to mildly unpleasant, yet their taste is considered good, and their tactile impression is “feathery.” Meanwhile, their characteristic sound is aptly summarized as “tweet.” In contrast, cats and dogs both share a tactile impression of “soft,” but differ in other respects: while cats are said to smell “fine,” their taste is not appreciated (“ew”); dogs, on the other hand, are perceived to smell anywhere from “fine” to “bad,” and their taste also elicits an “ew” response—a possible nod to subjective palatability or cultural context. Their typical vocalizations, “meow” for cats and “woof” for dogs, further reinforce familiar auditory associations.

Fish stand out in this grouping. Not only is their smell described unequivocally as “bad,” but their taste evokes a wide spectrum—from “very bad” to “very good.” This striking variation may reflect divergent culinary traditions or preparation methods. The tactile description “yuck” suggests a generally negative physical impression, and the absence of a typical sound (“---”) highlights their relative silence in everyday human-animal interactions.

It is also notable that the accompanying entries extend beyond direct sensory characterization. A series of rows contain fragmented references to spreadsheet functions, such as trial lookups, alongside notes on formula removal and error troubleshooting. Their presence, unconnected to animal characteristics, suggests remnants of data exploration rather than substantive contributions to the sensory profiles.

