| D&L          |                 430   |                 456.8 |
| SELLING EXP  |                 530.6 |                 532.2 |
| G&A          |                 669.7 |                 988.5 |
| OP. PROFIT   |                -610.5 |                -141.4 |
, one observes that overall sales increased significantly from the first to the second period. In January, sales totalled 6,340.1, rising to 7,573 in February. This represents a substantial uptick of approximately 19.4%, indicative of robust demand or successful sales initiatives.

Turning to costs, each major operating expense category also experienced increases, though the magnitude and rate of change varied. Material expenditures grew from 2,895.7 to 3,332.4, paralleling the sales growth and suggesting steady production scaling. Labour costs exhibited a modest rise, with a movement from 598.2 to 637.7. Overhead costs related to cost of sales, however, declined marginally from 1,826.4 to 1,766.8, hinting at possible efficiency improvements or altered allocation methods.

Distribution and logistics expenses (D&L) increased slightly from 430 to 456.8, while selling expenses showed near stability, moving from 530.6 to 532.2. General and administrative costs saw the most considerable relative escalation, jumping from 669.7 in January to 988.5 in February—a 47.6% increase, which could reflect either one-off items or expanding organizational requirements.

Operating profit remains negative for both months, but the loss narrowed from -610.5 to -141.4. This 76.8% reduction in operating deficit is notable—driven both by sales growth and a relatively contained increase in direct costs, despite the significant rise in G&A expenses.

If one were to envision a hypothetical performance benchmark, consider the following illustrative table summarizing typical monthly targets for a comparably sized enterprise:

