An initial examination shows that descriptor “a” is both highly prevalent and frequently occupies multiple descriptor columns within the same row. Notably, there is a single entry in which “a” extends fully across five columns, and another where it appears only in the primary descriptor field. Descriptor “b” demonstrates a similar pattern but achieves its maximal spread over four columns in its most complete instantiation. By contrast, descriptor “c” is distinctive for achieving full presence across all six columns in one instance—an indication of its potential comprehensiveness as an item type.

The sequence of rows that follow these complete entries predominantly display single descriptors, suggesting subsequent listings of each item type with no further elaboration in secondary columns. This pattern could reflect an initial set of exemplars, followed by repetitions focused solely on the primary identity of each item. Inspection of the table below illustrates these groupings and the distribution of descriptors across columns: ## Sheet: Sheet1

| item_desc   | item_desc1   | item_desc2   | item_desc3   | item_desc4   | item_desc5   |
|:------------|:-------------|:-------------|:-------------|:-------------|:-------------|
| a           | a            | a            | a            | a            | nan          |
