=== Report ===
An examination of handicap adjustment thresholds reveals distinct categorization for men’s and ladies’ handicap limits, each with corresponding buffer zones and adjustment rates. These classifications are designed to ensure equitable treatment across varying skill levels while maintaining responsiveness to changes in player performance.

For men, handicap categories range from 1 through 4, delineated by intervals of handicap values beginning at 0.1 and extending to 28. Within each category, the buffer zone—the range of nett differentials within which no adjustment occurs—increases incrementally from 1 to 4. When the player’s nett differential falls below zero, a calculated reduction per stroke is applied, progressively intensifying with higher categories; it starts at 0.1 and escalates to 0.4. Conversely, when nett differentials surpass the buffer zone, a minor increase in handicap typically follows, mostly set at 0.1, except for the highest category, where no increase is applied.

The pattern for ladies’ handicap limits is similar, albeit with a broader span of categories (1 through 6) and an extended range of handicap intervals that begin similarly at 0.1 but rise to 45 at the highest tier. Buffer zones correspondingly widen up to 5 in category 5, with the final category having no specified buffer. Reduction per stroke increments from 0.1 up to 1 at the highest handicap category, indicating a steep penalty for dropping below zero evaluation in this range. The increment for nett differentials exceeding the buffer remains consistent at 0.1 across most categories, except category 6, where it levels off to zero.

Taken together, these structures represent a nuanced system for adjusting handicaps based on performance fluctuations, incorporating both protective buffer zones and calibrated adjustment intensities that escalate with handicap magnitude.

