=== Report ===
A comparative analysis of fluid properties reveals intricate relationships between physical characteristics and visual attributes across various substances. Examining the spectrum from water to creme, a progression emerges in terms of density and viscosity: water, possessing the lowest density (0.457), also exhibits comparatively higher viscosity (3.55) at an elevated temperature of 500 units. As one moves through oil, gas, wine, and beer to creme, both density and viscosity tend to increase incrementally, while the associated sample temperature decreases steadily from 500 down to 150.

This inverse relationship between temperature and viscosity aligns with general physical principles, where lower temperatures correspond with increased fluid viscosity. For instance, creme, tested at the lowest temperature (150), is the densest (0.835) and least viscous (2.38) within the valid entries. In contrast, water is measured at the highest temperature and displays the lowest density among the group.

A visual representation of these fluids' properties, including their distinguishing colors, can be seen in ## Sheet: Sheet1

